      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the sum of 201088 and 978393. Hmm, addition problems can sometimes be tricky, especially when the numbers are big. Let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, I remember that adding two numbers can be done by aligning them vertically, right? So, writing them one under the other with the same number of digits. Let me try that. \n\nStarting with 201088 and 978393. Since 201088 has five digits and 978393 has six digits, I need to add a zero to the end of 201088 to make it six digits. That would make it 2010880. Then, adding the two numbers:\n\n```\n  2010880\n+ 978393\n-----------\n```\n\nLet me add them column by column from right to left. Starting with the units place:\n\n8 + 3 = 11. Write down 1 and carry over 1.\n\nNext, the tens place: 8 + 9 + 1 (carryover) = 18. Write down 8 and carry over 1.\n\nHundreds place: 0 + 7 + 1 (carryover) = 8. Write down 8.\n\nThousands place: 1 + 8 + 1 (carryover) = 10. Write down 0 and carry over 1.\n\nTen-thousands place: 0 + 7 + 1 (carryover) = 8. Write down 8.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 2 + 9 + 1 (carryover) = 12. Write down 2 and carry over 1.\n\nMillions place: 0 + 0 + 1 (carryover) = 1. Write down 1.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the sum is 1179481. Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 8 + 3 = 11. 11 is 1 in the units place and 1 in the tens place. Correct.\n\nTens: 8 + 9 + 1 (carryover) = 18. 18 is 8 in the tens place and 1 in the hundreds place. Correct.\n\nHundreds: 0 + 7 + 1 = 8. Correct.\n\nThousands: 1 + 8 + 1 = 10. 0 in the thousands place and 1 in the ten-thousands place. Correct.\n\nTen-thousands: 0 + 7 + 1 = 8. Correct.\n\nHundred-thousands: 2 + 9 + 1 = 12. 2 in the hundred-thousands place and 1 in the millions place. Correct.\n\nMillions: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1. Correct.\n\nSo, adding all the digits together: 1 + 1 + 7 + 9 + 4 + 8 + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2, 2 + 7 = 9, 9 + 9 = 18, 18 + 4 = 22, 22 + 8 = 30, 30 + 1 = 31, 31 + 1 = 32. Wait, that's 32? But let me recount the digits again.\n\nWait, the sum is 1179481. Let me count each digit:\n\n1 (millions), 1 (hundred-thousands), 7 (ten-thousands), 9 (thousands), 4 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 1 (units). Yes, that's 7 digits. So, 1179481. Hmm, that seems right. Let me verify once more.\n\nAdding 201088 and 978393:\n\n201088\n+ 978393\n= 1179481\n\nYes, that's correct. So, the sum is 1,179,481.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's a different way to approach this. Maybe using place value. Let's see. 201088 is 2 million, 0 thousand, 1 hundred, 8 tens, 8 ones. 978393 is 9 million, 7 hundred-thousands, 8 ten-thousands, 3 thousands, 9 hundreds, 3 tens, 9 ones. Adding them:\n\nMillions: 2 + 9 = 11. So, 11 million. Then, ten-thousands: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 ten-thousands. Then, thousands: 0 + 7 = 7. So, 7 thousands. Hundreds: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 hundreds. Tens: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 tens. Ones: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 ones. So, 11,117,117. Wait, that's 11 million, 11 ten-thousands, 17 thousands, 11 hundreds, 11 tens, 17 ones. But that's 11,117,117. But the original numbers were 201088 and 978393. Wait, no, 201088 is 2 million, 10 thousand, 8 hundred, 8 ones. 978393 is 9 million, 7 hundred-thousands, 8 ten-thousands, 3 thousands, 9 hundreds, 3 tens, 9 ones. So, adding them:\n\nMillions: 2 + 9 = 11. So, 11 million. Then, ten-thousands: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 ten-thousands. Then, thousands: 0 + 7 = 7. So, 7 thousands. Hundreds: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 hundreds. Tens: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 tens. Ones: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 ones. So, 11,117,117. But the original numbers are 201088 and 978393. Wait, no, 201088 is 2 million, 10 thousand, 8 hundred, 8 ones. 978393 is 9 million, 7 hundred-thousands, 8 ten-thousands, 3 thousands, 9 hundreds, 3 tens, 9 ones. So, adding them:\n\nMillions: 2 + 9 = 11. So, 11 million. Then, ten-thousands: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 ten-thousands. Then, thousands: 0 + 7 = 7. So, 7 thousands. Hundreds: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 hundreds. Tens: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 tens. Ones: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 ones. So, 11,117,117. But the original numbers are 201088 and 978393. Wait, no, 201088 is 2 million, 10 thousand, 8 hundred, 8 ones. 978393 is 9 million, 7 hundred-thousands, 8 ten-thousands, 3 thousands, 9 hundreds, 3 tens, 9 ones. So, adding them:\n\nMillions: 2 + 9 = 11. So, 11 million. Then, ten-thousands: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 ten-thousands. Then, thousands: 0 + 7 = 7. So, 7 thousands. Hundreds: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 hundreds. Tens: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 tens. Ones: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 ones. So, 11,117,117. But the original numbers are 201088 and 978393. Wait, no, 201088 is 2 million, 10 thousand, 8 hundred, 8 ones. 978393 is 9 million, 7 hundred-thousands, 8 ten-thousands, 3 thousands, 9 hundreds, 3 tens, 9 ones. So, adding them:\n\nMillions: 2 + 9 = 11. So, 11 million. Then, ten-thousands: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 ten-thousands. Then, thousands: 0 + 7 = 7. So, 7 thousands. Hundreds: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 hundreds. Tens: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 tens. Ones: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 ones. So, 11,117,117. But the original numbers are 201088 and 978393. Wait, no, 201088 is 2 million, 10 thousand, 8 hundred, 8 ones. 978393 is 9 million, 7 hundred-thousands, 8 ten-thousands, 3 thousands, 9 hundreds, 3 tens, 9 ones. So, adding them:\n\nMillions: 2 + 9 = 11. So, 11 million. Then, ten-thousands: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 ten-thousands. Then, thousands: 0 + 7 = 7. So, 7 thousands. Hundreds: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 hundreds. Tens: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 tens. Ones: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 ones. So, 11,117,117. But the original numbers are 201088 and 978393. Wait, no, 201088 is 2 million, 10 thousand, 8 hundred, 8 ones. 978393 is 9 million, 7 hundred-thousands, 8 ten-thousands, 3 thousands, 9 hundreds, 3 tens, 9 ones. So, adding them:\n\nMillions: 2 + 9 = 11. So, 11 million. Then, ten-thousands: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 ten-thousands. Then, thousands: 0 + 7 = 7. So, 7 thousands. Hundreds: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 hundreds. Tens: 8 + 3 = 11. So, 11 tens. Ones: 8 + 9 = 17. So, 17 ones. So