      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the sum of 723,460 and 189,100. Hmm, addition problems can sometimes be tricky, especially when dealing with large numbers. Let me start by writing them down to visualize the numbers better.\n\nFirst, let's align the numbers by their place values. Since both numbers have the same number of digits, I don't need to add extra zeros. So:\n\n723,460\n+ 189,100\n----------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (the units place), I'll add each corresponding digit. Let me go step by step.\n\nUnits place: 0 + 0 = 0\nTens place: 6 + 0 = 6\nHundreds place: 6 + 1 = 7\nThousands place: 4 + 8 = 12\nTen-thousands place: 3 + 9 = 12\nHundred-thousands place: 2 + 1 = 3\n\nWait, so adding up each place value:\n\nUnits: 0 + 0 = 0\nTens: 6 + 0 = 6\nHundreds: 6 + 1 = 7\nThousands: 4 + 8 = 12\nTen-thousands: 3 + 9 = 12\nHundred-thousands: 2 + 1 = 3\n\nNow, let's write down the results:\n\n3\n12\n12\n7\n6\n0\n\nSo, putting it all together, the sum is 312,760. Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n6 + 0 = 6\n6 + 1 = 7\n4 + 8 = 12\n3 + 9 = 12\n2 + 1 = 3\n\nYes, that seems right. So the sum is 312,760. Let me verify by adding the numbers another way to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, I can add the numbers column by column, starting from the rightmost digit.\n\n723,460\n+ 189,100\n----------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units place):\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n\nNext, tens place: 6 + 0 = 6\n\nHundreds place: 6 + 1 = 7\n\nThousands place: 4 + 8 = 12\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 + 9 = 12\n\nHundred-thousands place: 2 + 1 = 3\n\nSo again, the sum is 312,760. That matches the previous result. So I think that's correct.\n\nWait, just to be thorough, let me add the numbers step by step again.\n\nFirst, add the units digits: 0 + 0 = 0. That's straightforward.\n\nNext, tens digits: 6 + 0 = 6. Okay.\n\nHundreds digits: 6 + 1 = 7. That's another addition.\n\nThousands digits: 4 + 8 = 12. So 12 in the thousands place.\n\nTen-thousands digits: 3 + 9 = 12. So 12 in the ten-thousands place.\n\nHundred-thousands digits: 2 + 1 = 3. So 3 in the hundred-thousands place.\n\nSo putting it all together, the sum is 312,760. Yep, that seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can use the standard addition method. Let's try that.\n\nAlign the numbers:\n\n723,460\n+ 189,100\n----------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 + 0 = 0\n\n6 + 0 = 6\n\n6 + 1 = 7\n\n4 + 8 = 12\n\n3 + 9 = 12\n\n2 + 1 = 3\n\nSo writing that down:\n\n3\n12\n12\n7\n6\n0\n\nYes, same result. So that confirms it again.\n\nI think that's solid. The sum is 312,760. I don't see any mistakes in the process. Both methods of adding column by column and standard addition give the same result. So I can be confident that the answer is correct.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the sum of \\(723,460\\) and \\(189,100\\), we align the numbers by their place values and add each corresponding digit:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  723,460 \\\\\n+ 189,100 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units place):\n\n\\[\n0 + 0 = 0\n\\]\n\nNext, tens place:\n\n\\[\n6 + 0 = 6\n\\]\n\nHundreds place:\n\n\\[\n6 + 1 = 7\n\\]\n\nThousands place:\n\n\\[\n4 + 8 = 12\n\\]\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\n\\[\n3 + 9 = 12\n\\]\n\nHundred-thousands place:\n\n\\[\n2 + 1 = 3\n\\]\n\nPutting these results together, the sum is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{312760}\n\\]\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
