ng the carried over 1 gives 5. So, 5 in tens, 5 in hundreds. That's right.\n\nHundreds place: 7 + 0. 7 + 0 is 7. Then, adding the carried over 1 gives 8. So, 8 in hundreds, 8 in thousands. Correct.\n\nThousands place: 8 + 6. 8 + 6 is 14. So, 4 in thousands, 1 in ten-thousands. That's right.\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 + 4. 3 + 4 is 7. Then, adding the carried over 1 gives 8. So, 8 in ten-thousands, 8 in hundred-thousands. That's correct.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 6 + 4. 6 + 4 is 10. So, 0 in hundred-thousands, 1 in million. Wait, but the original numbers are 638,728 and 460,527. Let me check if the hundred-thousands place is 6 + 4. Yes, 6 + 4 is 10. So, 0 in hundred-thousands, 1 in million. That's correct.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the sum is 1,099,255. Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make any arithmetic errors.\n\n638,728 + 460,527\n\nAdding 638,728 to 460,527:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. 8 + 7 = 15. 5 in ones, 1 in tens.\n\n2. 2 + 2 = 4. 5 in tens, 5 in hundreds.\n\n3. 7 + 0 = 7. 8 in hundreds, 8 in thousands.\n\n4. 8 + 6 = 14. 4 in thousands, 1 in ten-thousands.\n\n5. 3 + 4 = 7. 8 in ten-thousands, 8 in hundred-thousands.\n\nSo, 8 4 8 4 1 8. Yes, that's 1,099,255. Hmm, seems right. Let me verify with another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, I can add the numbers column by column, starting from the right.\n\nStarting from the ones place: 8 + 7 = 15. 5 in ones, 1 in tens.\n\nTens place: 2 + 2 = 4. 5 in tens, 5 in hundreds.\n\nHundreds place: 7 + 0 = 7. 8 in hundreds, 8 in thousands.\n\nThousands place: 8 + 6 = 14. 4 in thousands, 1 in ten-thousands.\n\nTen-thousands place: 3 + 4 = 7. 8 in ten-thousands, 8 in hundred-thousands.\n\nHundred-thousands place: 6 + 4 = 10. 0 in hundred-thousands, 1 in million.\n\nSo, 1,099,255. Yep, that's the same result. So, I think that's correct.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's a possibility of a carry-over in the ten-thousands place. Let's see:\n\nIn the ten-thousands place, we have 3 + 4 = 7. Then, adding the carried over 1 gives 8. So, 8 in ten-thousands, 8 in hundred-thousands. That's correct.\n\nIn the hundred-thousands place, 6 + 4 = 10. So, 0 in hundred-thousands, 1 in million. That's correct.\n\nSo, the sum is indeed 1,099,255. I don't see any mistakes in the calculation. Let me just check once more:\n\n638,728 + 460,527\n\nAdding 638,728 to 460,527:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. 8 + 7 = 15. 5 in ones, 1 in tens.\n\n2. 2 + 2 = 4. 5 in tens, 5 in hundreds.\n\n3. 7 + 0 = 7. 8 in hundreds, 8 in thousands.\n\n4. 8 + 6 = 14. 4 in thousands, 1 in ten-thousands.\n\n5. 3 + 4 = 7. 8 in ten-thousands, 8 in hundred-thousands.\n\nSo, 8 4 8 4 1 8. Yes, that's 1,099,255. Seems right.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can add the numbers step by step:\n\n638,728 + 460,527\n\nFirst, add 638,728 and 460,527.\n\nLet me break it down:\n\n638,728 + 460,527\n\nStart from the right:\n\n1. 8 + 7 = 15. 5 in ones, 1 in tens.\n\n2. 2 + 2 = 4. 5 in tens, 5 in hundreds.\n\n3. 7 + 0 = 7. 8 in hundreds, 8 in thousands.\n\n4. 8 + 6 = 14. 4 in thousands, 1 in ten-thousands.\n\n5. 3 + 4 = 7. 8 in ten-thousands, 8 in hundred-thousands.\n\nSo, 8 4 8 4 1 8. Yes, that's 1,099,255. So, the answer is 1,099,255.\n\nI think that's correct. Let me just check if there's a different way to approach this problem, maybe using place value blocks or another method, to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the column addition method again, but maybe with a different approach. Let's try adding the numbers step by step.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n1. 8 + 7 = 15. 5 in ones, 1 in tens.\n\n2. 2 + 2 = 4. 5 in tens, 5 in hundreds.\n\n3. 7 + 0 = 7. 8 in hundreds, 8 in thousands.\n\n4. 8 + 6 = 14. 4 in thousands, 1 in ten-thousands.\n\n5. 3 + 4 = 7. 8 in ten-thousands, 8 in hundred-thousands.\n\nSo, 8 4 8 4 1 8. Yes, that's 1,099,255. So, the answer is 1,099,255.\n\nI don't see any mistakes in the calculation. Let me just check if there's a possibility of a carry-over in the ten-thousands place. Let's see:\n\nIn the ten-thousands place, we have 3 + 4 = 7. Then, adding the carried over 1 gives 8. So, 8 in ten-thousands, 8 in hundred-thousands. That's correct.\n\nIn the hundred-thousands place, 6 + 4 = 10. So, 0 in hundred-thousands, 1 in million. That's correct.\n\nSo, the sum is 1,099,255. I think that's the right answer.\n\n<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the sum of \\(638,728\\) and \\(460,527\\), we align the numbers vertically and add each column from right to left:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}\n & 6 & 3 & 8 & 7 & 2 & 8 \\\\\n+ & 4 & 6 & 0 & 5 & 2 & 7 \\\\\n\\hline\n & 8 & 4 & 8 & 4 & 1 & 8 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n1. **Ones place**: \\(8 + 7 = 15\\). Write \\(5\\) in the ones place and carry \\(1\\) to the tens place.\n2. **Tens place**: \\(2 + 2 = 4\\). Write \\(5\\) in the tens place and carry \\(1\\) to the hundreds place.\n3. **Hundreds place**: \\(7 + 0 = 7\\). Write \\(8\\) in the hundreds place and carry \\(1\\) to the thousands place.\n4. **Thousands place**: \\(8 + 6 = 14\\). Write \\(4\\) in the thousands place and carry \\(1\\) to the ten-thousands place.\n5. **Ten-thousands place**: \\(3 + 4 = 7\\). Write \\(8\\) in the ten-thousands place and carry \\(1\\) to the hundred-thousands place.\n6. **Hundred-thousands place**: \\(6 + 4 = 10\\). Write \\(0\\) in the hundred-thousands place and carry \\(1\\) to the million place.\n\nSummarizing the results:\n- Ones: \\(5\\)\n- Tens: \\(5\\)\n- Hundreds: \\(8\\)\n- Thousands: \\(4\\)\n- Ten-thousands: \\(8\\)\n- Hundred-thousands: \\(8\\)\n- Million: \\(1\\)\n\nThus, the sum is \\(\\boxed{1099255}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
