      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the sum of 813,964 and 353,147. Hmm, adding two big numbers like this can be tricky, but maybe I can break it down into smaller parts to make it easier. Let me start by writing them out:\n\n813,964\n+ 353,147\n----------\n\nFirst, let's line them up by their place values. Since both numbers have the same number of digits, I can add them column by column from right to left. Let me write them again with spaces to make it clearer:\n\n813,964\n+ 353,147\n----------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (the units place):\n\nUnits place: 4 + 7 = 11. So, 11 in the units place. But since we can't have more than 9 in a single place, we need to carry over 1 to the tens place. So, 11 becomes 1 in the units place and 1 in the tens place.\n\nNext, tens place: 6 + 4 = 10. Again, carry over 1 to the hundreds place. So, 10 becomes 0 in the tens place and 1 in the hundreds place.\n\nHundreds place: 9 + 3 = 12. Again, carry over 1 to the thousands place. So, 12 becomes 2 in the hundreds place and 1 in the thousands place.\n\nThousands place: 3 + 5 = 8. No carryover here because we didn't carry over from the previous step.\n\nTen-thousands place: 1 + 3 = 4. No carryover here either.\n\nTen-millions place: 8 + 0 = 8. No carryover here.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the sum should be 1,167,111. Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make any mistakes.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n4 + 7 = 11. 11 is 1 in units and 1 in tens. Correct.\n\n6 + 4 = 10. 10 is 0 in tens and 1 in hundreds. Correct.\n\n9 + 3 = 12. 12 is 2 in hundreds and 1 in thousands. Correct.\n\n3 + 5 = 8. Correct.\n\n1 + 0 = 1. Correct.\n\nSo, adding all those up: 800,000 + 100,000 = 900,000; 10,000 + 1,000 = 11,000; 1,000 + 100 = 1,100; 100 + 10 = 110; 10 + 1 = 11. Then, 110 + 110 = 220; 220 + 110 = 330; 330 + 110 = 440; 440 + 110 = 550; 550 + 110 = 660; 660 + 110 = 770; 770 + 110 = 880; 880 + 110 = 990; 990 + 110 = 1,100; 1,100 + 110 = 1,210; 