      "index": 0,
      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the sum of 613,480 and 868,360. Hmm, adding two big numbers like this can be tricky, but maybe I can break them down into smaller parts to make it easier. Let me start by writing them out:\n\n613,480\n+ 868,360\n----------\n\nFirst, let's line them up by their place values. Both numbers have four digits each, so I can add them column by column from right to left. Let me write them again with spaces to make it clearer:\n\n613,480\n+ 868,360\n----------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (the units place):\n\nUnits place: 8 (from 868,360) + 0 (from 613,480) = 8. So that's 8.\n\nNext, the tens place: 6 (from 868,360) + 8 (from 613,480) = 14. But since we're adding, we need to carry over 1 to the next column. So that's 14, which becomes 4 in the tens place and 1 in the hundreds place.\n\nMoving to the hundreds place: 3 (from 868,360) + 4 (from 613,480) + 1 (carryover) = 8. So that's 8.\n\nNext, the thousands place: 4 (from 868,360) + 3 (from 613,480) + 1 (carryover) = 8. So that's 8.\n\nThen, the ten-thousands place: 1 (from 868,360) + 1 (from 613,480) + 1 (carryover) = 3. So that's 3.\n\nFinally, the hundred-thousands place: 6 (from 868,360) + 6 (from 613,480) = 12. But since we're adding, we need to carry over 1 to the millions place. So that's 12, which becomes 2 in the hundred-thousands place and 1 in the millions place.\n\nPutting all these together, the sum should be 1,481,840. Let me check that again to make sure I didn't make any mistakes.\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nUnits: 8 + 0 = 8\n\nTens: 6 + 8 = 14, carryover 1\n\nHundreds: 3 + 4 + 1 = 8\n\nThousands: 4 + 3 + 1 = 8\n\nTen-thousands: 1 + 1 + 1 = 3\n\nHundred-thousands: 6 + 6 = 12, carryover 1\n\nMillions: 1 + 1 + 1 = 3\n\nSo yes, 1,481,840. Let me verify by adding the numbers step by step:\n\n613,480\n+ 868,360\n----------\n\nAdding 613,480 + 868,360:\n\nStart from the right:\n\n8 + 0 = 8\n\n6 + 6 = 12, carryover 1\n\n3 + 8 = 11, carryover 1\n\n4 + 3 = 7\n\n1 + 1 = 2\n\nSo total is 1,481,840. Yep, that seems right. I think that's the answer.<|end_of_thought|>\n\n<|begin_of_solution|>\n\nTo find the sum of \\(613,480\\) and \\(868,360\\), we align the numbers by their place values and add column by column:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n  613,480 \\\\\n+ 868,360 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (units place):\n\n\\[\n8 + 0 = 8\n\\]\n\nNext, tens place:\n\n\\[\n6 + 8 = 14 \\quad \\text{(carryover 1)}\n\\]\n\nHundreds place:\n\n\\[\n3 + 4 + 1 = 8\n\\]\n\nThousands place:\n\n\\[\n4 + 3 + 1 = 8\n\\]\n\nTen-thousands place:\n\n\\[\n1 + 1 + 1 = 3\n\\]\n\nHundred-thousands place:\n\n\\[\n6 + 6 = 12 \\quad \\text{(carryover 1)}\n\\]\n\nMillions place:\n\n\\[\n1 + 1 + 1 = 3\n\\]\n\nSumming all the results:\n\n\\[\n3 \\, 000, 000 + 1 \\, 481, 840 = 1, 481, 840\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of \\(613,480\\) and \\(868,360\\) is \\(\\boxed{1481840}\\).\n\n<|end_of_solution|>",
