      "text": "<|begin_of_thought|>\n\nOkay, let's see. I need to find the sum of 921126 and 866649. Hmm, addition problems can sometimes be tricky, especially when the numbers are big. Let me think about how to approach this step by step.\n\nFirst, I remember that adding two numbers can be done by aligning them vertically, right? So, writing them one under the other, making sure the digits are in the same place value. That way, I can add each column from right to left, starting from the units place.\n\nLet me write them out:\n\n  921126\n+ 866649\n----------\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit (the units place):\n\n6 + 9 = 15. But wait, since we're adding two numbers, we need to carry over if the sum is 10 or more. So, 6 + 9 is 15, which is more than 10. So, I'll write down 5 and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nNow, moving to the next column (the tens place):\n\n2 + 6 + 1 (the carryover) = 9. So, 2 + 6 is 8, plus the carryover 1 makes 9. So, write down 9.\n\nNext column (hundreds place):\n\n1 + 6 + 1 (carryover) = 8. So, 1 + 6 is 7, plus the carryover 1 is 8. Write down 8.\n\nNext column (thousands place):\n\n1 + 6 + 1 (carryover) = 8. So, 1 + 6 is 7, plus the carryover 1 is 8. Write down 8.\n\nNext column (ten-thousands place):\n\n2 + 6 + 1 (carryover) = 9. So, 2 + 6 is 8, plus the carryover 1 is 9. Write down 9.\n\nNext column (hundred-thousands place):\n\n9 + 8 + 1 (carryover) = 18. So, 9 + 8 is 17, plus the carryover 1 is 18. Write down 8, and carry over 1 to the next column.\n\nWait, but the next column is the ten-million place. Let me check again. The original numbers are 921126 and 866649. So, when adding, the first number is 921126, which is 9 ten-million, 2 million, 1 hundred thousand, 1 ten thousand, 2 thousands, and 6 hundreds. The second number is 866649, which is 8 ten-million, 6 million, 6 hundred thousand, 6 ten thousand, 4 thousands, and 9 hundreds. So, when adding them, the ten-million place is 9 + 8 = 17, which is 1 ten-million and 7 hundred-thousands. Then, the next column is the million place: 2 + 6 = 8, plus the carryover 1 is 9. Then, the hundred-thousands place: 1 + 6 = 7, plus the carryover 1 is 8. Then, the ten-thousands place: 1 + 6 = 7, plus the carryover 1 is 8. Then, the thousands place: 2 + 6 = 8, plus the carryover 1 is 9. Then, the hundreds place: 1 + 6 = 7, plus the carryover 1 is 8. Then, the tens place: 6 + 4 = 10, plus the carryover 1 is 11. Then, the units place: 6 + 9 = 15, plus the carryover 1 is 16. So, writing all these down:\n\nTen-million: 17 (1 ten-million and 7 hundred-thousands)\nMillion: 9 (8 million and 1 hundred-thousand)\nHundred-thousands: 8 (7 hundred-thousands and 8 ten-thousands)\nTen-thousands: 8 (8 ten-thousands and 8 thousands)\nThousands: 9 (9 thousands and 9 hundreds)\nHundreds: 8 (8 hundreds and 8 tens)\nTens: 11 (11 tens and 1 units)\nUnits: 16 (16 units and 16 tenths)\n\nSo, putting it all together, the sum is 17,988,985. Let me verify that. 17,988,985. Let's check each digit:\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,988,985\n\n17,98